# How a nerve signal travels
subtitle: "How a nerve cell fires an all-or-nothing spike, passes it down its axon like a burning fuse, and signals strength by rate."

## How a nerve signal travels
use: neuron
with: {"box":{"x":20,"y":330,"w":960,"h":360},"labels":false}
sources:
    - id: hh1952
      title: A quantitative description of membrane current and its application to conduction and excitation in nerve
      authors: Hodgkin AL, Huxley AF
      year: 1952
      finding: Explained the action potential in the squid giant axon by voltage-dependent sodium and potassium currents.
      doi: 10.1113/jphysiol.1952.sp004764

    path thr dashed #8f887c points [[200,215],[800,215]] hidden true
    text v_thr "−55 mV threshold" #8f887c x 890 y 217 font_size 24 hidden true
    path ap smooth #b5482e points [[200,180],[345,180],[378,199.2],[409,300],[440,199.2],[471,168],[539,175.2],[645,180],[800,180]] hidden true
    text v_rest "−70 mV" #3d3833 x 130 y 180 font_size 24 hidden true
    text v_peak "+30 mV" #b5482e x 470 y 300 font_size 24 hidden true
    portrait portrait_alan_hodgkin cites ["hh1952"] x 905 y 250 width 130 of "Alan Hodgkin" cameo false hidden true
    text L_dend "Dendrites" #5a534c x 80 y 600 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_soma "Cell body" #5a534c x 330 y 440 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_axon "Axon" #5a534c x 720 y 472 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_myel "Myelin" #5a534c x 611 y 472 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_node "Node" #5a534c x 452 y 472 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_term "Terminals" #5a534c x 840 y 612 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_imp "Signal" #b5482e x 561 y 615 font_size 26 hidden true
    text L_next "Next cell" #5a534c x 930 y 425 font_size 26 hidden true
    path nextcell smooth #3d3833 points [[925,580],[902,545],[898,515],[902,485],[925,450]] hidden true
    path hop283 smooth dashed #b5482e points [[346,537],[399,572],[452,537]] hidden true
    path hop384 smooth dashed #b5482e points [[452,537],[505,572],[558,537]] hidden true
    path hop485 smooth dashed #b5482e points [[558,537],[611,572],[664,537]] hidden true
    path hop586 smooth dashed #b5482e points [[664,537],[717,572],[770,537]] hidden true
    path t_light #b5482e points [[200,240],[290,242],[295,258],[300,315],[305,258],[312,232],[325,238],[510,242],[515,258],[520,315],[525,258],[532,232],[545,238],[730,242],[735,258],[740,315],[745,258],[752,232],[765,238],[800,240]] hidden true
    path t_firm #b5482e points [[200,140],[230,142],[235,158],[240,215],[245,158],[252,132],[265,138],[300,142],[305,158],[310,215],[315,158],[322,132],[335,138],[370,142],[375,158],[380,215],[385,158],[392,132],[405,138],[440,142],[445,158],[450,215],[455,158],[462,132],[475,138],[510,142],[515,158],[520,215],[525,158],[532,132],[545,138],[580,142],[585,158],[590,215],[595,158],[602,132],[615,138],[650,142],[655,158],[660,215],[665,158],[672,132],[685,138],[720,142],[725,158],[730,215],[735,158],[742,132],[755,138],[800,140]] hidden true
    text L_light "light touch" #3d3833 x 110 y 250 font_size 24 hidden true
    text L_firm "firm press" #3d3833 x 110 y 150 font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "How a nerve signal travels"

How does a signal travel along a nerve cell? It starts at the dendrites, the branches that collect messages from other cells.
    draw dendrites L_dend

The cell body adds them up.
    draw soma nucleus L_soma

At rest, its inside sits at minus seventy millivolts, charged like a tiny battery.
    draw v_rest

If the inputs lift it to about minus fifty-five, the threshold, the neuron fires. Below that, nothing is sent at all.
    draw thr v_thr

Firing means sodium channels open, sodium ions rush in, and the inside flips to about plus thirty, for roughly a millisecond.
    draw ap v_peak

    pause 0.4

That spike happens first where the axon begins: the long output cable.
    draw axon L_axon

Now the inrush of charge lifts the next patch of membrane past threshold, so it fires too, then the next, like a fuse burning along.
    flow axon

A current in a wire fades with distance. But this signal doesn't: every patch rebuilds it to full size, so it arrives as strong as it left.
    highlight axon

And it runs one way only. A patch that has just fired needs a moment to reset, so the wave cannot turn back.
    draw impulse L_imp

Myelin speeds it up. It wraps the axon in insulation, so the spike is rebuilt only at the gaps, the nodes.
    draw myelin L_myel L_node

In between, it leaps from node to node, which is far faster.
    draw hop283 hop384 hop485 hop586

At the terminals, finally, it releases chemical messengers that pass the signal to the next cell.
    draw terminals L_term nextcell L_next

Every spike is the same size. So how does a neuron say 'stronger'?
    highlight ap

    erase ap v_peak thr v_thr v_rest parallel true

It fires more often. A light touch sends a few spikes a second;
    draw L_light t_light

a firm press sends many more.
    draw L_firm t_firm

Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley worked this out in 1952, in a squid's giant axon, about half a millimetre thick. It won them a Nobel Prize.
    draw portrait_alan_hodgkin

    erase portrait_alan_hodgkin

    quiz "You press harder on your fingertip. What changes in the signal its nerve sends?"
        right: More spikes per second: each spike is all or nothing, always the same size and speed, so strength is sent as how often it fires.
        * The spikes get bigger
        + There are more spikes per second
        * The spikes travel faster
---
